{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To find the smallest distance between the origin and a point on the graph of \\( y = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x^2 - 3) \\), we need to minimize the distance function. The distance \\( D \\) from the origin \\((0,0)\\) to a point \\((x, y)\\) on the graph is given by:\n\n\\[\nD = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( y = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x^2 - 3) \\) into the distance formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nD = \\sqrt{x^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x^2 - 3) \\right)^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nD = \\sqrt{x^2 + \\frac{1}{2}(x^2 - 3)^2}\n\\]\n\nExpand \\((x^2 - 3)^2\\):\n\n\\[\n(x^2 - 3)^2 = x^4 - 6x^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\nD = \\sqrt{x^2 + \\frac{1}{2}(x^4 - 6x^2 + 9)}\n\\]\n\nDistribute \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\nD = \\sqrt{x^2 + \\frac{1}{2}x^4 - 3x^2 + \\frac{9}{2}}\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\nD = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}x^4 - 2x^2 + \\frac{9}{2}}\n\\]\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we minimize the function inside the square root. Let:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{2}x^4 - 2x^2 + \\frac{9}{2}\n\\]\n\nFind the critical points by taking the derivative of \\( f(x) \\) and setting it to zero:\n\n","prefix_end":1043,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,1477,279,24632,6010,1948,279,6238,323,264,1459,389,279,4771,315,17767,379,284,1124,37018,90,16,15170,59,26888,90,17,3417,7,87,61,17,481,220,18,8,1124,701,582,1184,311,29337,279,6010,729,13,576,6010,17767,422,1124,8,504,279,6238,1124,1188,15,11,15,10699,8,311,264,1459,1124,1188,87,11,379,10699,8,389,279,4771,374,2661,553,1447,59,9640,35,284,1124,26888,45340,61,17,488,379,61,17,532,59,2533,3136,3696,10607,17767,379,284,1124,37018,90,16,15170,59,26888,90,17,3417,7,87,61,17,481,220,18,8,1124,8,1119,279,6010,14806,11,582,633,1447,59,9640,35,284,1124,26888,45340,61,17,488,1124,2359,7,1124,37018,90,16,15170,59,26888,90,17,3417,7,87,61